In article <JLM.95Sep27162853@ludo.two-oo-one.fr>
jlm@ludo.two-oo-one.fr (Jean-Louis Moser) writes:
jlm> | May be here is a bug in DEC C++ V5.0-3 for DEC OSF/1 (Alpha).

Nope, don't think so.

jlm> void foo (float a, ...)

jlm> void main ()
jlm> {
jlm>     foo (1.0f,2.0f,3.0f,4.0f,0);
jlm> }

As in C, any argument for which there is no parameter is subject to
the usual default promotions, so (regardless of what you may have
intended) 2.0f, 3.0f and 4.0f are passed as doubles, not floats.  The
call should be

foo (1.0f,2.0f,3.0f,4.0f,0.0);

0 won't do -- it has to be the length of a double, so an int is too
short.  The type given to va_arg must be the type after any promotions
have been applied, so the values should be retrieved with

    a = va_arg (list, double);

-- 
