Alberto Menegazzi <flash.egon@iol.it> wrote:
>Hi, I'm a Linux user. I would like to know where does Linux stands in
>the galaxy of all the UNIXes.
>
>Is it binary compatible with other UNIXes for intel platforms ? Which
>are these other UNIXes for Intel ?

No it is not.

>Are the BSDs some of those ? Why does three different BSDs (normal,
>Free and Net) exist?

BSD is the "original unix" as it were.  It was ported to the Intel chips both
as a public port (BSD386) and as various commercial projects (eg, BSDi).

The people who did the original public port disappeared, and two different
teams of programmers, with different areas of interest, continued to work on
the code.  This produced two different flavors of public BSD for the Intel
chips.  (FreeBSd and NetBSD)

>What is SunOS? Is it different from Solaris? Does an Intel version
>exist?

SunOS is Sun's version of BSD, for their machines.  Solaris is sort of "the
next version" of SunOS, although it is so very different than its predecessor
that it is really a different OS entirely.  Solaris is more SYSV'ish, and a
version does exist for Intel chips.

>Is AIX a UNIX ? What kind ?

Yes.  It is IBM's unix.  I have heard it described as "a high speed collision
between BSD and SYSV".  I'd add "as overseen by Big Blue" to that.

>Does Silicon Graphic use a UNIX ?

Yes, Irix.  Correct me if I'm wrong, but I think it's a SYSV system.

>What UNIXes are SVR3.2 compatible and what are SVR4 compatible ?

No clue.

>Is MACH, the kernel on which NEXTSTEP operating system should be based
>on, a UNIX ?

Sort of.  Arguments could be made either way :)

>What kind of UNIX is HP-UX I used on HP 9000/715 in my university ?

SYSV, as overseen by HP.

>waht is OpenSystemFoundation ?

A coalition of manufacturers who wanted to take the current zoo of unixes and
make a more standard standard.  I think that this is better explained in the
FAQ. 

>Is Plan9 from AT&T a UNIX ?

I've heard of Plan9 from Outer Space, but not from AT&T.  I don't know.

>Does any FAQ about these questions exist?

The OSF FAQ from this group can't hurt.  I'd also read comp.unix,
comp.unix.misc, and/or comp.unix.questions' FAQ's.

	Alec

